
## The layers

- **The structure is declared in `.config/rotproof.toml`, and `rotproof check` fails when the tree differs from it,
  either way.** Delete an unused layer and list it in `absent`; to bring one back, remove it from `absent` and run
  `rotproof create --yes`, which makes only what is missing (without `--yes`, it lists the layers it would make and
  writes nothing). Code outside every layer fails too: move it into a layer, or
  list its path in `unchecked` (helper scripts, generated code). Code of the project that is not in a layer yet is
  listed in `moving` instead: no check reads it, and while a path is listed `rotproof check` prints how many files are
  left, which to move next and the layers each could go in (not `domain`, `protocol`, `application` or
  `presentation`, for a file that would do I/O there or import a package no list allows there, in Python and
  TypeScript). Move them into the layers in the order it gives, and choose among the layers that pass by what the code
  is for; tests stay where they are, so a path of tests in `moving` fails. It lists, too, every import of such a path
  (`unchecked` as well) by those four layers, which no check judges: a helper moved there is not a fix.{not_layers}
{direction}

{table}
- **`application` and `infrastructure` meet only in `protocol` (dependency inversion).** A use case implements an input
  port of `protocol.inbound` and calls the output ports of `protocol.outbound`; an entry point in
  `infrastructure.inbound` calls only the input ports, and a connection in `infrastructure.outbound` implements an
  output port. `composition` alone builds the implementations and wires them, and the entry file only calls it. Define
  a port only when it is called, with only the methods called.
- **A vocabulary (the kinds a value may have, with no rule of the domain in it) is written once, in the unit's own
  `domain`, and `protocol.inbound` re-exports it:** it is the one thing `protocol.inbound` imports of its own unit's
  `domain`, by name, from the file that defines it, which the table does not show, and `rotproof check` fails any
  other import of it. The entry points, the UI and the tests use the values the port gives and never write a list of
  their own, so a vocabulary has values at run time, not only a type. It holds no rule: a decision that depends on the
  kind is a function of `domain` that takes the kind. A record is never imported so: write the shape the port shows
  in `protocol.inbound`, and convert in `application`, since a record changes for other reasons than the port does.

{vocabulary}
- **A part goes in the layer whose concept it speaks.** No layer holds general-purpose parts: a general procedure comes
  from the standard library or an external one, and wanting to write one's own is a sign of a concept not named yet.
- **A new import the table forbids passes only as an approval, and an approval is asked for by the user.** Fix a
  forbidden import. Run `rotproof approve <file> <import>... --reason "<why>"` only when the user asked you to keep
  it (a framework forces it, a fix cannot wait for the refactoring), and say so in your report, with the reason: it
  adds each import to `.config/rotproof-approved.toml`, signed with git's `user.name`, and asks for nothing else. The
  imports of one file kept for one reason go in one run, which writes all of them or, when one cannot be approved,
  none.
  `rotproof check` prints every approval on every run, fails on one that matches no forbidden import any more (after
  fixing an approved import, run `rotproof approve --prune`, which removes such entries), and fails when the
  approvals are more than `approved_max` in `.config/rotproof.toml` lets the project hold, if it has one. Raising
  `approved_max` is the user's decision: never add, raise or remove it to pass the check.
- **A baseline is written once, when the user asks, and only shrinks.** Run `rotproof baseline` only when the user asked
  you to freeze what still fails (it refuses while `moving` lists a path, and when the file is there): it writes
  `.config/rotproof-baseline.toml` with every forbidden import and every use of I/O of the inner layers that no
  approval covers, and the count of them. `rotproof check` passes what is in it, fails on any other violation, prints on
  every run how many entries remain, fails on an entry that matches nothing (after fixing one, run `rotproof baseline
  --prune`, which removes such entries and lowers the count), and fails on more entries than the count. Never run
  `rotproof baseline` again, never add an entry, and never raise `count` to pass the check: a file written again larger
  is a new violation let through, and the diff shows it. A new violation is fixed, or approved when the user asks.
{units}{rules}{ui}